4.Methodolgy
  Data Strcuture: Each matrix stores only non-zeros, as flat arrays of {int r; int c; double val;} triplets
                  of length exatly nnz. No dense row-pointer strcuture remains anywhere in the code.


  Sequential Baseline:  B's entries are bucketed by row (counting sort) so each row is a contiguous slice. For every non-zero a₍ᵢₖ₎ 
                        of A we emit one triplet (i, j, a₍ᵢₖ₎·b₍ₖⱼ₎) per non-zero in row k of B, with the total count pre-computed before allocation.
                        Products are sorted by (row, col), equal coordinates are summed, exact zeros are dropped, and the result is compacted in place. 
                        This output is the ground truth.



  Parallel Engine: A shred counter hands out rows of A to worker threads one at a time - a dynamix row queue. Real sparse
                  matruces have irregular row densities, so a static split would leave threads holding dense rows while others idle;
                  the queue naturally rebalnces. Each claimed row is accumulated entirely in thread-local buffers, so no shared data
                  is touched during the multiplicatuon loop itself - no locks guard individual products, satisfying the assignements
                  race-safety requirement. Finished rows merge into the shared output under one lock acquisition per roww.
  
  Correctness: For each thread count {1, 2, 4, 8, 16}, the parallel result is compared cell-by-cell against the sequential reult at 
              EPSILON = 1e-7, in both directions - cells missing from the parallel output and "phantom" cells represents only
              in it both count as failures. Both results are first folded into hsh maps keyed b (row, col) that sum duplicate entries,
              making the comparison O(nnz) instead of O(nnz^2) - essential for matrices with millions of non-zeros and independent of 
              storage order.
  Timing: Wall-clock time via CLOCK_MONOTONIC, wrapped around the engie call only - file loading is exculded. Each configuration runs
          N >= 3 times. Speedup = sequential time / best parallel time

